## Why This Is Asked
Production CNPA systems require robust observability to diagnose performance issues during peak loads. This tests practical knowledge of distributed systems debugging and real-world monitoring strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Distributed tracing with OpenTelemetry
- Context propagation across service boundaries
- Tail latency analysis and SLA management
- Performance monitoring and alerting thresholds
- Load testing and canary deployment strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// OpenTelemetry configuration for CNPA pipeline
const { NodeSDK } = require('@opentelemetry/sdk-node');
const { HttpInstrumentation } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-http');
const { PgInstrumentation } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-pg');
const { RedisInstrumentation } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-redis');

const sdk = new NodeSDK({
  instrumentations: [
    new HttpInstrumentation(),
    new PgInstrumentation(),
    new RedisInstrumentation()
  ],
  serviceName: 'cnpa-service'
});

// Kafka context propagation example
const produceWithTracing = async (topic, message, span) => {
  const headers = {
    traceparent: span.spanContext().traceparent,
    'x-request-id': span.spanContext().traceId
  };
  await producer.send({ topic, messages: [{ value: message, headers }] });
};
```

## Why This Is Asked
Aimed to assess mastery of end-to-end guarantees across HTTP API, DB, Kafka, and Redis, including failure handling and read-model integrity.

## Key Concepts
- Outbox pattern
- Kafka transactional producers
- Idempotent consumers
- Cross-service failure handling
- Read model consistency and testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// PostgreSQL transaction: write user update and outbox row
BEGIN;
UPDATE users SET name = $1 WHERE id = $2;
INSERT INTO outbox (aggregate_type, aggregate_id, event_type, payload, sent)
VALUES ('user', $2, 'UserUpdated', '{id:' + $2 + ',name:' + $1 + '}', false);
COMMIT;
```

```javascript
// Pseudo publisher
async function publishOutbox() {
  const rows = await fetchUnsentOutbox();
  const txn = await kafka.beginTransaction();
  for (const r of rows) {
    await kafka.send({ topic: 'user.events', key: r.aggregate_id, value: r.payload }, txn);
  }
  await markOutboxSent(rows.map(r => r.id));
  await txn.commit();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement idempotent apply of events in Redis and read models?
- How would you simulate outages to ensure no duplicates or gaps?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical cache-coherence strategies under CNPA load, covering Redis locking, API backpressure, and Kafka pacing with realistic validation.

## Key Concepts
- Cache stampede prevention
- Redis distributed locking (SETNX, EX) and TTL refresh
- Per-key rate limiting and circuit breakers
- Backpressure across HTTP, Redis, and Kafka
- Observability and staged rollout validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js sketch for cache refresh with Redis lock
const lockKey = `lock:${cacheKey}`;
if (await redis.set(lockKey, '1', 'NX', 'EX', 30)) {
  const value = await loadFromDBOrCompute(cacheKey);
  await redis.set(cacheKey, value, 'EX', 300);
  await redis.del(lockKey);
} else {
  // another worker refreshes
  await sleep(50);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor lock contention and adjust TTLs at scale?
- How would you test resilience when Redis cluster experiences partial outages?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design end-to-end observability and actionable debugging plans for critical checkout flows, ensuring latency budgets are met under peak load and across service boundaries.

## Key Concepts

- Distributed tracing (OpenTelemetry), trace context propagation (traceparent, baggage)
- End-to-end latency budgets and tail latency analysis
- Per-layer metrics: HTTP, DB (latency, queue time), Kafka (producer/consumer lag), Redis (cache hit rate)
- Sampling strategies that balance overhead and signal strength
- Debugging steps: slow query logs, partition lag, cache invalidation timing, idempotent sinks
- Concrete fixes: connection pools, prepared statements, cache invalidation, backpressure handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// OpenTelemetry setup example: propagate trace context across HTTP and DB drivers
const { NodeTracerProvider } = require('@opentelemetry/sdk-trace-node');
const { SimpleSpanProcessor } = require('@opentelemetry/sdk-trace-base');
const { ConsoleSpanExporter } = require('@opentelemetry/exporter-console-span');

const provider = new NodeTracerProvider();
provider.addSpanProcessor(new SimpleSpanProcessor(new ConsoleSpanExporter()));
provider.register();

// Instrumentations (pseudo-imports; actual setup may vary by stack)
require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-http');
require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-pg');
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate end-to-end plan under load and which SLA metrics would you lock to?
- How would you adjust sampling and instrumentation overhead in production without losing signal?
